  <!-- DETAILED FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS (FAQS) -->
  <!-- ========================================================= -->
  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900" open>
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          What is the minimum clear cover required for an RCC footing?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          As per <strong>IS 456:2000 Cl. 26.4.2.2</strong>, the minimum nominal cover for reinforcement in isolated footings is <strong>50 mm</strong> when placed on a lean concrete (PCC) blinding bed. If the footing is poured directly against earth without blinding concrete, the minimum clear cover increases to <strong>75 mm</strong>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          Why are vertical L-bends necessary at the ends of footing mesh bars?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Footings experience maximum bending moment at the column face, and shear stresses are highest near the edges. Standard 90° upward $L$-hooks (height equal to <em>D − 2×Cover</em>) provide full development anchorage length ($L_d$) so the rebars do not slip under peak flexural loads.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          Why is a 100mm PCC blinding bed laid before pouring the footing?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Plain Cement Concrete (PCC M10 or M7.5, 1:3:6 / 1:4:8) provides a smooth, hard, level foundation bed. It prevents soil moisture from contaminating structural RCC concrete, guarantees uniform 50mm cover block placement, and stops cement slurry from leaking into the ground.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          How do I calculate starter dowel bars for columns?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Column starter dowels must have the same or larger diameter than the column's vertical rebars. They require an embedment into the footing depth, a horizontal 90° bend of at least 300 mm resting on and tied to the bottom mesh, and an upward projection of at least 50 times the bar diameter ($50d$) to form a seismic lap zone with column rebars.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

